<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nanjing Massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rape of Nanking, Nanking Massacre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> Massacre was the mass killing, rape and looting carried out by Japanese troops after they captured Nanjing, then the capital of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in December 1937. Over about six weeks, tens of thousands and by many estimates hundreds of thousands of prisoners and civilians were killed. It remains one of the most notorious atrocities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nanjing-massacre.jpg" alt="Nanjing Massacre" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Originally Moriyasu Murase, 村瀬守保 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanking_bodies_1937.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> began on 7 July 1937, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge">Marco Polo Bridge</a> incident. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in November, its troops advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, and along the 170 miles between the two cities they committed atrocities as part of a terror campaign, including a reported killing contest between two officers. General Iwane Matsui commanded the Central China Area Army, and Prince Yasuhiko Asaka was installed as temporary commander and issued an order to kill all captives. The Chinese garrison of some 81,500 soldiers, many of them untrained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, was gathered under Tang Shengzhi. The government left the city in early December, leaving its administration to an International Committee led by the German John Rabe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Japanese forces reached the city gates on 9 December, and the garrison collapsed on the night of 12 December. The massacre is dated from 13 December, when Japanese troops entered the city. They killed civilians, among them children, women and the elderly, and executed thousands of captured soldiers and men wrongly taken for soldiers. The International Military Tribunal for the Far East estimated about 20,000 rapes in the first month; other estimates range from 4,000 to 80,000. One third of the city was burned. The first three weeks were the most intense, and atrocities in the area continued until late March 1938.</p><p>A committee of foreigners had set up the Nanking Safety Zone in the western quarter of the city, which is credited with saving at least 200,000 lives. The Dane Bernhard Arp Sindberg and the German Karl Gunther turned a cement factory into a refuge for 6,000 to 10,000 people. Eyewitnesses included Rabe, the missionaries John Magee, who filmed and photographed events, and Lewis S. C. Smythe, and the journalist Frank Tillman Durdin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Estimates of the dead differ widely, partly because the Japanese army destroyed records and disposed of corpses. The tribunal's figure was more than 200,000; newer estimates fall between 100,000 and 200,000; the range runs from 40,000 within the city to over 340,000 across the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> officially commemorates 300,000.</p><p>Matsui was convicted for failing to stop the crimes and, with Kōki Hirota and five others, hanged on 23 December 1948. Asaka was granted immunity as a member of the imperial family. Hisao Tani was tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and shot on 26 April 1947. The Memorial Hall for the victims opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 1985. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, some officials and a few historians deny or minimise the massacre, and it remains a point of contention in relations with China.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nanjing Massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese armies lost the Battle of Shanghai, Nanjing fell in December 1937 amid the <b>Nanjing Massacre</b>, and the government withdrew to Chongqing, where it held out until 1945 at a cost of millions of military and civilian lives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Military</a></li></ul></section>
